<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mishneh Torah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1170">1170</a>–<a href="/years/1180">1180</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Sefer Yad ha-Hazaka, Book of the Strong Hand</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mishneh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> is a code of Rabbinic Jewish religious law (halakha) by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a>, compiled between 1170 and 1180 CE while he was living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and regarded as his magnum opus. Written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and divided into fourteen books, it is the only medieval-era work that details all of Jewish observance, including the laws that apply only when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a> is in existence. Maimonides intended a complete statement of the Oral Law, so that a reader who had mastered the Written Torah and this code would need no other book. Early critics objected to its absence of sources and feared it would supersede study of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a>, but it remains an important work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mishneh-torah.jpg" alt="Mishneh Torah" width="330" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maimonides (text); Isaac (copist), Matteo di Ser Cambio (illumination) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rambam_mishne-torah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The title Mishneh Torah means &quot;repetition of the Torah&quot; and was originally a name for the biblical book of Deuteronomy. The work is also called Sefer Yad ha-Hazaka, the &quot;Book of the Strong Hand&quot;, because fourteen, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> letters as yod and dalet, spells yad, hand. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a> compiled it between 1170 and 1180 while he was living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. He wrote it in Hebrew, as the Mishnah had been, since he was reluctant to use Talmudic Aramaic, which was not widely known; his earlier works were in Judeo-Arabic. He intended a complete statement of the Oral Law, so that someone who had mastered the Written Torah and then this work would need no other book.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The fourteen books are divided into sections, chapters and paragraphs. The first, HaMadda (Knowledge), covers belief in God, proper conduct, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> study, idolatry and repentance, and further books treat love of God through prayer and blessings, the times of the Sabbath and festivals, and the laws of marriage and divorce. The work sets out all of Jewish observance, including the laws that apply only while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a> stands. The book almost never cites sources or arguments and states the final ruling for each case, with chapters following the subject matter rather than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a>'s order. It also contains a widely quoted list of eight levels of charitable giving.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The work met strong opposition almost as soon as it appeared, chiefly because it gave no sources and seemed meant to replace study of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a>. The most influential critic was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> ben <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> of Posquières, whose comments are printed beside nearly every edition. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a> answered that he wanted brevity and a needed code, that he had not meant to end Talmud study, and that he regretted not adding sources. Yonah of Gerona took part in burning copies of the Sefer ha-Madda in the 1240s but repented after Talmud volumes were burned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1244. The work soon won recognition as an authority for ritual decisions, and Meir HaKohen adapted it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi</a> readers in the Haggahot Maimuniyyot.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mishneh Torah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…wrote the most widely used commentary on the Bible and Talmud; Moses Maimonides, in 12th-century Cairo, wrote the <b>Mishneh Torah</b>, a code of all Jewish law, and the Guide for the Perplexed, a work of philosophy; Joseph Karo’s Shulchan Aruch of 1565 became the standard code of practice.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars such as Rashi, in 11th-century France, wrote commentaries on the Bible and Talmud still studied today, and Maimonides wrote his great code of Jewish law, the <b>Mishneh Torah</b>, in clear Hebrew in the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/language/hebrew">Hebrew · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
